{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And he said: Swear to me.</b> The reason an oath was required is that within the birthright there are particulars that are intangible things, such as honor and distinction, which Jacob was intent upon for the service of the house of our Elohim that is included in the birthrights. And according to our holy Torah, a person acquires neither by sale nor by gift a thing that has no tangible substance, as is known (Rambam, Laws of Sale, chapter 22). Therefore he devised to gain title to the matter by means of an oath. And so it is the consensus of the codifiers of the Torah (Yoreh De'ah, siman 239) that an oath takes effect both upon a thing that has tangible substance and upon a thing that has no tangible substance — and this is the meaning of his saying <b>Swear to me.</b>" "Now, this oath would not suffice for acquisition of a thing not yet in existence, for the reason that a person cannot acquire a thing not yet in existence is because the buyer's mind is not relied upon, and so it is proven in Bava Metzi'a folio 16. But if his mind were settled upon it, it would be acquired, and an oath would not help for this. Therefore the word ״כַיּוֹם״ (\"as of this day\") was required, as I have explained. Subsequently there came into my hands Sefer HaRivash, siman 328, and this is its language: \"You wrote that you saw Rabbi Yaakov ben HaRosh who wrote in the name of his father that one who acquires a thing not yet in existence — if he swore, he has acquired it, etc., and his proof is from the sale of the birthright that Esav sold to Yaakov, where it is written ״הִשָּׁבְעָה לִּי״,\" end quote. Response: Neither the Rosh nor Rabbi Yaakov his son signed their names to it, and one should not attribute to great authorities a reasoning such as this that has nothing to support it. Moreover, the proof they brought has no foundation, because this was before the giving of the Torah, and who is to tell us that a person could not acquire a thing not yet in existence then? And the oath was merely for the betterment of the matter, so that he would not renge, since he was a man of might — end quote. And these are our very own words, except that the Rivash did not reflect to give a reason why Esav's oath was required, as I have written." "<b>And the reason</b> he said <b>Swear to me</b> and did not suffice with saying ״הִשָּׁבְעָה״ — and the matter is self-evident, since the sale was his — is that he intended to say that this oath must be in accordance with Jacob's own mind, for without this he could annul it in his heart. And the essence of an oath must be in the heart, as Rabbi Akiva said (Sifra, chapter 12): the obligation of a person in an oath is that his mouth and his heart must be equal at the time of the oath. Therefore he said ״הִשָּׁבְעָה לִּי״ — meaning: according to my mind." "<b>Furthermore,</b> he was precise in saying <b>to me</b>, for it is specifically he [Jacob] in whom the sale of the birthright can be fulfilled in all its parts, but not in anyone else. For anyone else would acquire only things that are monetary, but things that are inherited distinctions from their father — such as the service in the house of Elohim and the honor — do not belong to a foreigner, and it is wholly beyond his capacity to sell them to an outsider." "<b>And he sold his birthright.</b> The Torah validated the sale, confirming that it was binding, for the reasons we have written — even though he sold only under compulsion, and even though he sold something worth a hundred maneh for one maneh. And this is the language of the Maharik (Choshen Mishpat 213): \"If he sold because of his distress and degraded the sale beyond what reason would tolerate — for a stranger who because of his distress was forced to underprice — he cannot retract, for he knew and forgave,\" end quote. From this it follows: even though he sold something vastly valuable for a single portion of stew, his sale is a sale, as he revealed his mind by saying, ״Behold, I am going to die״ — and there is no greater distress than this, (Job 2:4) ״עוֹר בְּעַד עוֹר וְכָל אֲשֶׁר לָאִישׁ יִתֵּן בְּעַד נַפְשׁוֹ״. And without this circumstance there would have been no way for Jacob to gain title to the birthright from Esav's hand. And concerning Esav the prophet said (Proverbs 5:22) ״עֲוֹנוֹתָיו יִלְכְּדֻנוֹ אֶת הָרָשָׁע״ — that because of the sins he committed that day, as they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 63:14) interpreting the verse ״וְהוּא עָיֵף״, this was the cause to remove the birthright from him and let it stand in the hand of the one worthy of it."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {4 "Maharik reference normalized to Choshen Mishpat; the Hebrew text's 'רכז' (213) likely stands for שי״ג in gematria but is rendered as printed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:39.474411643Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}